hypotheses.statement:
The entry choices can be made so that, for every fixed $0<c<c'<3$, uniformly for integers $c2^k\le j\le c'2^k$ as $k\to\infty$, $$\label{trj:unbalanced-conclusion}
 \begin{split}
 t_j^{(k)}&=\frac{1}{2(\log L)j}
                  +O\!\left(\frac{\log(2+j)}{j^2}\right),\\
 z_j^{(k)}&=O(j^{-1}),\qquad
 \|\mathcal R_j^{(k)}\|_{j,k}=O(j^{-2}).
 \end{split}$$ The constants may depend on $c,c'$ and the fixed map parameters. In particular the assertion applies at the stop $j'=N-O(\log N)$ in the history chosen for $2^k\le N<2^{k+1}$.

outputs.exact_statement:
For integer sequences $n,n'\to\infty$ with $n'/n\to d\in(0,\infty)$, $$\label{geo:center-ratio}
                  \frac{M_{n'}}{M_n}\longrightarrow d^{-1/8}.$$ There is a constant $c_{\rm shape}\in(0,\infty)$ such that $$\label{geo:bulk-ratio}
                  \frac{C_{b_c}(r)}{M_r^2}\longrightarrow c_{\rm shape}
                  \qquad(r\to\infty,\ r\in\mathbb Z).$$ Here $C_{b_c}$ is the thermodynamic limit in <a href="#eq:critical-definition" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:critical-definition">[eq:critical-definition]</a>; that limit precedes $r\to\infty$. In particular, $M_n=n^{-1/8+o(1)}$.

Let $U$ be a bounded connected polygonal Lipschitz domain in the fixed-geometry class of Proposition <a href="#prop:height-inputs" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:height-inputs">4</a>. Its outer boundary and the boundaries of its two holes are simple and rectilinear, with dyadic vertices and positive side lengths; the hole closures are disjoint and strictly inside the outer boundary, leaving positive-width passages. Let $\mathsf F$ be an aligned union of positive-area boxes with dyadic vertices in $U$, permitted as a pin in that fixed geometry. Suppose a simple dyadic rectilinear arc joins interiors of sides of the two holes transversely and has a neighborhood at positive distance from $\mathsf F$ and all nonincident boundaries. Give the holes periods $+2\pi,-2\pi$. Let $\vartheta=dv$ be a smooth closed one-form near $\overline U$ with these periods, with a multivalued primitive whose only jump is across that arc and whose branch is zero on a neighborhood of $\mathsf F$. Choose the canonical integral connections obtained from the jumps of this branch along a coherent rounded discretization of the arc, or connections related to them by integral gauges whose potentials vanish on every component of $\mathsf F$. Then, along every admissible rounded mesh sequence, $$\label{geo:pinned-sector-bound}
 \liminf_{\nu\to\infty}
       \Phi_{\mathcal G_\nu(U),\,h_{\mathsf F}=0}^\tau
       \ge \exp\left\{-\frac1{2a}\int_U|\vartheta|^2\right\}.$$

Assume every activity has period $\omega$ under common field translations, and its field dependence lies in the sites of its occupied support $X$ together with a fixed microscopic collar of width at most a fixed small fraction of $u$. The norm padding $D_u(X)$ is generally larger. Call an input small when its connected support has $|X|\le n_{\mathrm{sm}}$, for the fixed block threshold of the calculus. For a linear direction assume the point bound $$|F|_{u,X,\phi}\le
 \nu_X W_u^\kappa(X,\phi)e^{V_u(X,\phi)}
 \qquad\text{for every real }\phi,$$ with $\nu_X<\infty$; the resulting estimates are homogeneous in $\nu_X$.*

*For a sufficiently large fixed dyadic $L$, sufficiently large weights $A_w$, and a sufficiently small activity ball, shell integration and designated localizations on small linear inputs give the exact identity $$\label{eq:exact-local-map}
 \mathbb E_\zeta\mathcal Z_u(K;\phi+\zeta)
 =\prod_{B\in\mathcal B_{Lu}}(1+a_B)\,
        \mathcal Z_{Lu}(K';\phi).$$ The map is analytic in the activities. If the total input size, including separately measured singleton coefficients, is $\nu$, its nonlinear remainder is bounded by $C_{L,A_w}\nu^2$, with the corresponding differentiated bounds. A residual from a large input retains its whole coarse closure; only a piece from a small linear input can be assigned to a singleton. The coefficient $a_B$ is the complete sum of the field-independent pieces designated at $B$. Any undesignated constant value of a translated nonconstant piece remains in $K'$.*

*Write $\Gamma_u=C_{<Lu}-C_{<u}$ for the shell from $u$ to $Lu$. For its estimates on the aligned block and observation grids of a finite square or torus of side $M$, assume $Lu\le M/D_0$, with $D_0$ a sufficiently large fixed range buffer. The norms <a href="#eq:point-norm" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:point-norm">[eq:point-norm]</a>–<a href="#eq:activity-norm" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:activity-norm">[eq:activity-norm]</a> have the shell composition, localization, charge, and large-support contractions of [[B](#ref-B), Sections 4 and 8, Lemma 9.2]. In particular, the extra regulator is monotone under enlargement, adds on compatible separated supports, and costs $C^{|X|}$ under shell averaging. On the full bulk block grid, a small neutral input invariant under field negation has gain $CL^{-3}$ after constant and quadratic localization. Removing only the constant gives $CL^{-2}$ for a neutral input invariant under field negation and $CL^{-1}$ for a general neutral input, including at irregular end blocks. For a charge-$k$ component of a small input, $k\ne0$, the Gaussian charge gain is $$\exp\left\{
 -\bigl(\beta_{\mathrm{sh}}|k|\alpha-\tfrac12\beta_{\mathrm{sh}}^2\bigr)
       \Gamma_u(v_0,v_0)+h_0|k|\alpha\right\}.$$ Here a charge-$k$ component obeys $F(\phi+c)=e^{ik\alpha c}F(\phi)$, $v_0$ is a reference site in a block of $X$, and $\beta_{\mathrm{sh}}>0$ is the Cameron-shift amplitude. The analytic radius must satisfy $h_0>C_{\mathrm{sh}}\beta_{\mathrm{sh}}$, where $C_{\mathrm{sh}}$ is the fixed geometric constant for that shift, and the parameters are chosen so that $\beta_{\mathrm{sh}}\Gamma_u(v_0,v_0)>h_0$ by a uniform fixed margin. The displayed gains are then summable over $k\ne0$. This is [[B](#ref-B), Equation (4.26)]. Large inputs can be made an arbitrarily small linear contribution by increasing $A_w$.

The branches can be chosen bounded, with a consistent additive constant, and for $0\le r\le4$ they satisfy, in lattice units, $$\label{ent:unit-decay}
\begin{split}
 |\nabla^r\eta_n(e)|
   &\le C_r(1+\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(e,0))^{-1-r},\\
 |\nabla^r(\eta_n-\eta_\infty)(e)|
   &\le C_r n^{-1-r}
       \qquad\bigl(\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(e,0)\le c_0n\bigr),
\end{split}$$ where $c_0>0$ is fixed and sufficiently small. The first bound also holds for $\eta_\infty$. Differences at a free side use the reflected stencils of the local calculus. On a source-free rectangle with its required collar, there is a single-valued real lift $\lambda$ in reference-field units satisfying $$\nabla\lambda=\eta_n/\alpha .$$ Changing the integral gauge changes this lift by an integer multiple of the common period $\omega=2\pi/\alpha$.

There are $c>0$ and $s_1<\infty$, independent of the finite cell topology, such that for every $s\ge s_1$, every cut topology used below, and its centered sector or the restriction of the aligned unit sector, $$\label{ent:pointwise-cutoff}
 \frac{\widehat Z_G^\tau(y)}{Z_G^0(0)}
       \le \exp\left\{-c\sum_{f\in\mathcal F(G)}w_f^2\right\}
                    \qquad(y\hbox{ real}).$$*


*For a connected full finite graph, let $\mathcal P_G^\tau(F)$ denote the integral of $F(y)Z_G^\tau(y)$ on the observation space modulo $y\mapsto y+2\pi k\mathbf1$, $k\in\mathbb Z$, and let $\widehat{\mathcal P}_G^\tau(F)$ use $\widehat Z_G^\tau$. For the centered sector take either a uniformly bounded function $F$ on this quotient, or $F=\ell(y)^k e^{t\ell(y)}$ times a uniformly bounded function on the quotient, where $k$ is a fixed nonnegative integer, $t$ is in a fixed compact subset of $\mathbb R$, and the mean-free linear observation $\ell$ has uniformly bounded primary reference cost and noise variance. For the square unit sector take $F=1$. Then $$\label{ent:cutoff-loss}
 \frac{\left|\mathcal P_G^\tau(F)
             -\widehat{\mathcal P}_G^\tau(F)\right|}
      {\mathcal P_G^0(1)}
       \le C_F|\mathcal F(G)|e^{-cs^2}.$$ In particular, if the logarithm of the side is $O(N)$ and $s\asymp N$, this bound is at most $e^{-c'N^2}$ for some $c'>0$. The same conclusion holds for the difference of normalized centered averages of the stated tests.

For the entry family just described, there is a number $D_s$, the same at every full junction, and a symmetric matrix $H_G$ such that $$\label{ent:face-decomposition}
 \mathcal Q_G(w)=D_s\sum_{z\in\mathcal V_4(G)}\sigma_z(w)^2
             +\sum_{f,f'\in\mathcal F(G)}H_G(f,f')w_fw_{f'},
 \qquad 0\le D_s\le C\log(2+s).$$ The second matrix need not be positive. In cell distances it has the bounds $$\label{ent:face-matrix-bounds}
\begin{split}
 |H_G(f,f')|&\le Ce^{-c\,d(f,f')},\\
 |H_G(f,f')-H_{\widetilde G}(f,f')|
 &\le C\min\left\{
       e^{-c\,d(f,f')},
       e^{-c\,d(\{f,f'\},\mathcal D)}
                    \right\}.
\end{split}$$ In the second line $\mathcal D$ contains the cells and faces where the two topologies or their local boundary rules differ, enlarged by the fixed stencil radius of the local trial, forcing, and constant quadratic coefficients used below. Extend absent entries by zero on the oriented face set of one common uncut inclusion $U$, and measure all distances there. The two decays can be combined after decreasing $c$.*



*For the guarded comparison, let $E$ be the same still-retained local deletion in the current full graph and its current patch: $$G_1=G_0\setminus E,\qquad
 P_0=G_0|_O,\qquad P_1=G_1|_O=P_0\setminus E.$$ These four graphs belong to the stated tile and box family. The patch restrictions inherit every earlier cut and every retained observation row and local rule in $O$; their only new free wall is at the artificial boundary of $O$. On the common face set define $$\Delta=(H_{G_0}-H_{G_1})-(H_{P_0}-H_{P_1}).$$ Let $K_\Delta$ contain both before/after discrepancy sets and let $A_\Delta$ contain both full/patch discrepancy sets, including all absent exterior cells and faces and the artificial-boundary collar. Enlarge both by the same fixed stencil radius. If $d_U(K_\Delta,A_\Delta)\ge r$, then $$\label{ent:guarded-face-error}
 |\Delta(f,f')|
 \le C e^{-cr-c\,d_U(f,f')-c\,d_U(\{f,f'\},K_\Delta)}.$$ Shared physical free walls and earlier cuts are common data and do not belong to $A_\Delta$. Temporary hard primary guards are not among these four free-energy graphs. All constants are independent of $s$ and of the containing finite volume. For the physical centered or unit data this also gives $$\label{ent:face-energy-upper}
 E_G^\tau(y)\le C\sum_{f\in\mathcal F(G)}w_f^2
       +C\,1_{\{\text{unit junction is full in }G\}}\log(2+s).$$

Fix the finite list of map norms and reserves just described. There are constants $C,c>0$ such that the following holds for every sufficiently large fixed dyadic $R$, with $P=R^5$ and the fixed period $q$. For every sufficiently large dyadic $s$ satisfying <a href="#ent:entry-side" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ent:entry-side">[ent:entry-side]</a>, the assertions below hold uniformly in the finite volume. The threshold on $s$ may depend on all preceding choices. A compatible torus or aligned free square has side at least $sP$; for a marked square its side is larger than the fixed multiple of $sP$ needed to contain the compulsory region and its collars. In the square applications its ratio to $sP$ tends to infinity.*

1.  *There are plane activities $K_m^0$ which are real on real fields, negation-even, invariant under common translation by $\omega$, and covariant under block translations and square symmetries. There are centered torus and free-square activities $K_{\mathcal G,m}^0$ with $$\|K_m^0\|_{m,A_w,V},
                      \ \|K_{\mathcal G,m}^0\|_{m,A_w,V}
                             \le Ce^{-cR}$$ in each of the fixed norms. On every such finite graph a positive field-independent scalar $c_{\mathcal G,m}$ makes <a href="#ent:ordinary-subset" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ent:ordinary-subset">[ent:ordinary-subset]</a> hold. If $D_m(X)$ unwraps in a torus, its activity is exactly the plane activity. The same is true in a free square when $D_m(X)$ misses the wall.*


2.  *In a free square with the center unit there are real periodic activities $K_{\mathcal G,m}^\tau$ and $J_{\mathcal G,m}$ for which <a href="#ent:labelled-subset" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ent:labelled-subset">[ent:labelled-subset]</a> holds with that same scalar. For a constant $C_R<\infty$ independent of $s,n$, $$\label{ent:label-norm}
     \|K_{\mathcal G,m}^\tau\|_{m,A_w,V}\le Ce^{-cR},
     \qquad
     \|J_{\mathcal G,m}\|_{m,A_w,V}\le C_Rs^{C_R}.$$ The sector norm may use the slightly weaker weight and stronger regulator already reserved above.*

    *There is a fixed set $\mathfrak C_{\star,m}$ of at most $C_R$ entry blocks, containing the full inflation of a square of side a fixed large multiple of $P$ in observation-cell units around the source, such that $$\label{ent:compulsory-support}
           J_{\mathcal G,m}(X)=0
                      \quad\hbox{unless}\quad
                      \mathfrak C_{\star,m}\subset X.$$ Every block of $\mathfrak C_{\star,m}$ is occupied in $X$ in the subset functional. The full support of each summand of $J(X)$ also occupies every padded dependency and the complete connecting, winding, or wall-reaching tag of every item in that summand.*



3.  *If the rectangle spanned by $D_m(X)$ misses the source, then $$\label{ent:translated-copy}
     K_{\mathcal G,m}^\tau(X,\psi)
                 =K_{\mathcal G,m}^0(X,\psi+\lambda_X),$$ where $\lambda_X$ is its local lift. This assertion uses the finite-square centered prescription if the support is near a wall. There are also centered-unit plane prescriptions $K_{\infty,m}^\tau$ and $J_{\infty,m}$. Whenever $D_m(X)$ misses the square wall under the centered aligned identification just specified, its prescription is exactly the plane one with background translated by $$\label{ent:plane-background}
                             (v_n-v_\infty)/\alpha .$$ This identity holds for both the unlabelled and labelled activities. The sufficient regions in all three copy statements include the padded supports and all occupied tags. The plane centered-unit prescriptions obey the same bounds as in <a href="#ent:label-norm" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ent:label-norm">[ent:label-norm]</a>, with their local plane norms.

After the parameter choices in <a href="#ent:reserve-order" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ent:reserve-order">[ent:reserve-order]</a> and <a href="#ent:entry-side" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ent:entry-side">[ent:entry-side]</a>, and the threshold choice described below, there is $s_0<\infty$ such that for every admissible $s\ge s_0$, $$\label{ent:analytic-pattern-bound}
 |F_\pi|_{m,X,\psi}
 \le \mathcal C_\pi(s)\,
       C^{j_1+j_2}e^{-cR(j_1+j_2)}
       \prod_\ell Ce^{-c(R+d_\ell)}
       W_m^\kappa(X,\psi)e^{V_{j_0}(X,\psi)}$$ for all real backgrounds $\psi$. The point norm on the left is the full derivative sum in <a href="#eq:point-norm" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:point-norm">[eq:point-norm]</a>. The constants are uniform in the compatible tori and free squares.

The entry choices can be made so that, for every fixed $0<c<c'<3$, uniformly for integers $c2^k\le j\le c'2^k$ as $k\to\infty$, $$\label{trj:unbalanced-conclusion}
 \begin{split}
 t_j^{(k)}&=\frac{1}{2(\log L)j}
                  +O\!\left(\frac{\log(2+j)}{j^2}\right),\\
 z_j^{(k)}&=O(j^{-1}),\qquad
 \|\mathcal R_j^{(k)}\|_{j,k}=O(j^{-2}).
 \end{split}$$ The constants may depend on $c,c'$ and the fixed map parameters. In particular the assertion applies at the stop $j'=N-O(\log N)$ in the history chosen for $2^k\le N<2^{k+1}$.

Along the compatible even tori $n\to\infty$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E_n^{\rm tor}e^{w x_n}&\longrightarrow
                  e^{a\mathfrak g w^2/2},\label{trj:torus-laplace}\\
 \mathbb E_n^{\rm tor}[e^{i\bar h}e^{w x_n}]
   -\mathbb E_n^{\rm tor}e^{i\bar h}\,e^{a\mathfrak g w^2/2}
       &\longrightarrow0.\label{trj:torus-factorization}
\end{aligned}$$ The first assertion includes convergence of every fixed moment, and both assertions are locally uniform in complex $w$. They also hold for $X_{n,s},\chi_{n,s}$ when $s/n\to0$. For any prescribed small error in a fixed finite set of these diagnostics, one can first take $P$ sufficiently large and then $s$ sufficiently large so that the error is small uniformly over compatible $n\ge sP$.

For every sufficiently small prescribed tube, $R$ and the entry threshold can be chosen so that, for all sufficiently large $k$, every plane state through $j\le4\cdot2^k$ stays in that tube. For each $j\in\mathcal J_k$, the torus states at all stages $j_0(k)\le i\le j$ stay in the corresponding small ball. For every fixed $0<c<c'<4$, as $k\to\infty$, $$\label{trj:vanishing-ranges}
 \begin{split}
 \sup_{\substack{i\in\mathbb Z,\ j_0(k)\le i\\c2^k\le i\le c'2^k}}
 \bigl(|t_i^{(k)}|+|z_i^{(k)}|
                   +\|\mathcal R_i^{(k)}\|_{i,k}\bigr)&\longrightarrow0,\\
 \sup_{\substack{i\in\mathbb Z,\ j\in\mathcal J_k,\ j_0(k)\le i\le j\\
                  c2^k\le i\le c'2^k}}
 \bigl(|t_i^{(k)}|+|z_i^{(k)}|
                   +\|\mathcal R_{i|j}^{\rm tor,(k)}\|\bigr)&\longrightarrow0.
 \end{split}$$

Fix $0<c<c'<3$. Uniformly as $\min(k_1,k_2)\to\infty$, for every common integer $j$ satisfying $c2^{k_i}\le j\le c'2^{k_i}$ for $i=1,2$, put $(u_i,Y_i)=(u_j^{(k_i)},|Z_j^{(k_i)}|)$. Then $$\label{trj:overlap-bounds}
 \begin{split}
 |u_1-u_2|&\le C(Y_1^2+Y_2^2),\\
 |Y_1-Y_2|&\le C\sum_{i=1}^2(|u_i|+Y_i)Y_i.
 \end{split}$$ The constants may depend on $c,c'$ and the fixed parameters. In particular $Y_1/Y_2\to1$ uniformly under these conditions.

Fix $0<\epsilon<1/10$. For all sufficiently large $N$, put $n_0=L^N$, $n_1=L^{N+1}$, and choose a power $m'=L^{j'}$ so that $$\label{obs:stop}
 D_N=\frac{n_0}{m'}\asymp N^\epsilon,\qquad
 j'=N-O(\log N).$$ Use the entry history for $N$ in both squares, and let $t_{j'}$ be its unbalanced plane gradient coefficient at the stop. Then $$\label{obs:consecutive-formula}
 \log\frac{M_{n_1}}{M_{n_0}}
 =-\frac{1-t_{j'}}{2a}(g_{n_1}-g_{n_0})
   +O\left(\frac{D_N^4}{N^2}+\frac{1}{ND_N}\right).$$ Moreover, for a constant $c_G$, $$\label{obs:green-constant}
                g_{L^N}=2\pi(\log L)N+c_G+o(1).$$ All constants are independent of $N$.

The local maps can be chosen in both squares with the following properties. Here $\theta<1$ and $C$ depend only on the fixed parameters.*


1.  *Untwisted activities on wall-free supports are the exact plane activities, and $$\label{obs:wall-recurrence}
     w_{j+1}\le \theta w_j+C U_j+C(U_j+w_j)^2.$$*



2.  *On a shift-good support, $K_j^{\tau,n}$ is the corresponding untwisted activity evaluated at a local lift $\lambda_n$, with $\nabla\lambda_n=\eta_n/\alpha$. Near a wall this uses the finite-square untwisted activity. On defects, $$\label{obs:defect-recurrence}
     d_{j+1}\le\theta d_j+C(U_j+w_j)+C(U_j+w_j+d_j)^2.$$ On a central support whose complete causal neighborhood is wall-free in both squares, the unit activity is the common plane centered-unit prescription evaluated at $$\label{obs:smooth-background}
                         \phi+\delta_n,\qquad
              \delta_n=\frac{v_n-v_\infty}{\alpha}.$$*


3.  *The surviving label obeys $$\label{obs:label-recurrence}
     \|J_{j+1}\|_{j+1}
     \le \{\theta+C(U_j+w_j+d_j)\}\|J_j\|_j.$$ Every nonzero $J_j^n(X)$ has an occupied block within a fixed number of current blocks of the unit. Its support may extend farther. It has the same central plane-unit copy property as in <a href="#obs:smooth-background" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="obs:smooth-background">[obs:smooth-background]</a>.*




4.  *Choose $H_A$ in <a href="#obs:affected-set" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="obs:affected-set">[obs:affected-set]</a> sufficiently large. For every output block outside $\mathcal A_j$, in either square, $$\label{obs:factor-outside}
     a_{jB}^{\tau,n}=a_{jB}^{0,n},\qquad
                      \dot a_{jB}^{\tau,n}=0.$$ For every $B\in\mathcal A_j$, the complete sums satisfy $$\label{obs:factor-copy}
     a_{jB}^{0,n_0}=a_{jB}^{0,n_1},\qquad
     a_{jB}^{\tau,n_0}=a_{jB}^{\tau,n_1},\qquad
     \dot a_{jB}^{\tau,n_0}=\dot a_{jB}^{\tau,n_1}.$$ In the fixed small ball, choose $\rho<1$ with $|a_{jB}^{\sigma,n}|\le\rho$ for $\sigma\in\{0,\tau\}$. Then every output block satisfies $$\label{obs:scalar-log-bound}
     \left|\log\frac{1+a_{jB}^{\tau,n}}{1+a_{jB}^{0,n}}\right|
           \le C(U_j+w_j+d_j).$$

Write $$W_u^\kappa(X,\phi)=\exp\{\kappa U_u^{\rm reg}(X,\phi)\}.$$ Here $U_u^{\rm reg}$ is the sum of gradient energy on $D_u(X)$, the $u$-weighted boundary trace, and the $u^4$-weighted block maxima of squared second differences. Its domain contains all boundary endpoints and fixed difference stencils, with even extension at a free wall. Let $$\mathfrak R_u^{\rm b}(X,\phi)
       =W_u^{\kappa_{\rm b}}(X,\phi)e^{V_{u,h_{\rm b}}(X,\phi)}$$ be the regulator used to measure terminal activities, where $V_{u,h}$ denotes <a href="#eq:observation-regulator" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:observation-regulator">[eq:observation-regulator]</a> with $h_*=h$.*



*There is a fixed $1<p_{\rm seg}<p_1$ with the following property. For a support $X$ of $k$ terminal blocks, put $D=D_{m'}(X)$. Suppose a real deterministic field $h_X$, allowed to depend on $n$ and $X$, is defined on the full domain just described and satisfies $$\label{obs:terminal-translation}
 U_{m'}^{\rm reg}(X,h_X)+\langle h_X,A_Dh_X\rangle\le C_{\rm tr}k$$ with one uniform constant. Then $$\label{obs:terminal-moment}
 \mathbb E\bigl[\mathfrak R_{m'}^{\rm b}
                 (X,\zeta_{*,n}+h_X)^{p_{\rm seg}}\bigr]\le C^k.$$ The same bound holds at every point of a coupled segment between two such endpoints when their local regulator forms are identical. A random common anchor may be subtracted at each endpoint after neutral projection. The assertion for the segment is a bound on the moment at each parameter value, uniformly in that value.*



*There are $H_N=O_L(D_N^2)$ terminal blocks in either square. Suppose every unlabelled terminal activity has norm at most $\nu$ in the weakest norm and put $b_N=CH_N\nu$. If $b_N=o(1)$, its terminal partition integral $T$ satisfies $$\label{obs:terminal-expansion}
 T=1+S_1+O(b_N^2),\qquad |S_1|\le b_N,\qquad
                  \log T=S_1+O(b_N^2),$$ where $S_1$ is the sum of single-activity expectations. For a label direction of norm $v$, the derivative obeys $$\label{obs:terminal-derivative}
                         |\dot T|\le CH_Nv e^{b_N}.$$ These estimates allow the constituent-dependent translations in <a href="#obs:terminal-translation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="obs:terminal-translation">[obs:terminal-translation]</a>; their fixed per-block cost is absorbed by the weight.
